events=[('II.1.2.6.7.homotopy-adjunction','The ordinary adjunction (*) is compatible with path objects: the relative path object of B plus F over B is the pullback of (B plus F)^{Delta1} over B^{Delta1} along constant paths, hence B plus F^{Delta1}. Applying (*) to F^{Delta1} identifies homotopies with their two endpoint maps on both sides. Passing to the equivalence relation generated by homotopy therefore gives the bijection (**).'),('II.1.2.6.7.comparison-chain','Since the pro-system Omega(C/A) tensor_C B over fibrant quasi-isomorphisms C->B is essentially constant with value L^Delta(B/A), the definition of Hom from a pro-object gives the first displayed inductive limit. The bilateral fraction calculus for B-modules gives the second limit over target quasi-isomorphisms M->F. The two indexing categories are independent, so their iterated inductive limits commute. Apply (**) termwise; the relative right-fraction theorem I 3.1.8 (ii) prime then computes the inner C-limit as Hom_D(A-Alg/B)(B,B plus F). All maps B plus M -> B plus F are quasi-isomorphisms by the preceding page, so the outer diagram is naturally isomorphic to the constant Hom set with F=M. Its index category is nonempty and connected (identity M is initial), yielding the last isomorphism. Every comparison is natural in M. This completes the proof of Proposition 1.2.6.7 and identifies the correct citation in the third line.')]
